# 7. Advantages and Disadvantages

| **Advantages**                                                                       | **Disadvantages**                                                                  |
| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Zero licensing fees reduces capital overhead in large-scale cluster hosting.               | LTS releases contain older, frozen packages (requires custom PPAs for newest builds).    |
| Massive community support and robust documentation simplifies issue resolution.            | Software installer processes can occasionally lock, halting automated shell scripts.     |
| Regular, highly predictable security updates and Long-Term Support (LTS) guarantees.       | Snap-packaged utilities can cause slower application starts and higher disk consumption. |
| High native integration with popular DevOps, cloud provider, and containerization systems. | Frequent core upgrades can break legacy dependencies or custom built modules.            |

---

# 8. Best Practices

| **Best Practice**                           | **Description**                                                                                                |
| ------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Standardize on LTS Versions**             | Deploy Ubuntu LTS (Long-Term Support) versions in environments to guarantee security patches for up to 5 years.      |
| **Perform Index Updates Prior to Installs** | Run`sudo apt update` before software installations to prevent package dependency mismatch errors.                  |
| **Remove Orphaned Dependencies**            | Regularly invoke`sudo apt autoremove` and `sudo apt clean` to free up disk space and eliminate unused libraries. |
| **Run Daemon Services as Non-Root Users**   | Modify systemd service configurations to execute processes using isolated system accounts (e.g.,`User=nobody`).    |
| **Inspect System Logs Regularly**           | Utilize`journalctl -xe` and monitor `/var/log/syslog` to catch process failures and trace security events.       |

---

# 9. Recommendation / Conclusion

It is highly recommended to deploy **Ubuntu LTS (22.04 or higher)** using **APT** for software package management and **systemd** for daemon process supervision. This stack represents the industry standard for stability, container compatibility, security, and developer ergonomics, minimizing maintenance friction across CI/CD runners and server environments.

